Garbage
Seeing such a package, I ask everyone: don’t you think it looks like trash that just got thrown on the side of the road?
Is it responsible for this plastic shatterproof packaging to have been sent from Germany in a plastic bag?
I think this kind of packaging is the same as garbage, and I bought this product. The packaging is very simple, and there is no any measures to prevent damage. It is just a simple plastic bag packaging. After receiving it, the packaging is completely deformed, and the contents inside are also damaged.
If I had just encountered one of these products, I would have accepted it. I bought several products, and all of them were in the same condition. I contacted Amazon customer service, who said that I could return the products, but the shipping cost would have to be prepaid. The shipping cost was then refunded to me in the form of a gift card.
I’d like to ask: I pay for the shipping costs in cash, and you offer me a gift card as a refund. But if you want me to buy the gift card myself, just say so clearly. Moreover, I have to mail the product to the warehouse in Germany myself. Isn’t that just creating extra trouble for me simply because I want to purchase a product?
And I’ve been telling the customer service all along who is responsible for such problems, but they refuse to admit even a single word that it’s their fault—they don’t even dare to take any responsibility. What kind of after-sales service is this, then? In one word: they should just admit their mistake! But I guess Amazon won’t even give my review a chance to be published; they’ll just delete it directly.
This AV amplifier is complicated to set up, and the workmanship is rough, and it generates a lot of heat.
The sound needs to be used for a while before I can give a review!
I just bought the German version of the DN1080 on August 14th. At first glance, the quality of the exterior workmanship is extremely poor.
1. The front panel and all the knobs are made of plastic.
2. The audio input jack on the rear panel, as well as the speaker output terminals, are of very poor quality. Any CD/DVD player combo that costs between 1000 and 2000 yuan would be much better than this product.
3. The top cover is relatively thin, and the transformer side is sunken in, so I had to open the box and use my hand to restore it. The top cover and the front panel are loose.
4. The center of gravity of the entire machine is biased towards the transformer side, making it very awkward to move.
It feels like a basic entry-level model from SONY, just looking at the quality of construction; it’s produced in Malaysia.
It seems that it would be much better for Sony to have manufacturing in China done by local contractors rather than by companies like Xiaomi.
5. The machine generates a lot of heat. When using it to watch a movie in 2-channel mode for about 2 hours, the temperature of the top cover reaches 40–50°C when touched by hand. I’m not sure if this is because the transformer is too large and therefore produces more heat.
Nowadays, most use switch-mode power supplies; this big transformer must weigh at least 4 kg.
Sony should use passive cooling fans with silent operation inside the devices. Upon opening the box, all that was visible were large aluminum heat sinks attached to the pipes.
I don't feel too good.
First of all, the box isn’t very sturdy; it bends with just a little pressure. Secondly, the design on the back is intended to prevent the ABS material on the box’s surface from scratching when the box is placed flat, but it doesn’t really work effectively. Furthermore, the inside of the box is just covered with a piece of fabric, and the area where the handles are located is clearly visible… There are all sorts of issues with this product. Finally, the zipper operates a bit smoothly.
PS: The wheels really feel quite good.
Overall it’s good, but there are a few minor issues.
This is my first purchase of a water filter pitcher. After reading several review articles, I decided on the LAICA model due to its good cost-performance ratio. Upon receiving the product, I installed the filter cartridge according to the instructions and gave it a try. Overall, it seems to work well. However, when pouring water, it appears that the raw water mixes with the filtered water at the spout of the pitcher (the seal of the raw water storage compartment is not very tight).
Be sure to wait until all the water has been filtered out before pouring it!
Pretty good, but with some minor disappointments.
The color of the entire cup is something I really love—orange-red. Although I have a baby boy, I still think that orange-red combined with yellow looks very striking.
It’s a bit smaller than I expected, but since it’s meant to be used outdoors anyway, it’s still acceptable.
I took it home last night to give it a try. After opening the bottle cap, the distance between the straw and the cap was too short. Since the straw is positioned vertically in the correct direction, my son always has to hold the protrusion on the cap with his mouth while drinking water. I wonder if this aspect could be improved somehow.
It seems that other brands’ straw cups have some sense in making their straws inclined; after all, it’s kids stuff and they really do care about safety and comfort.
It’s okay; for a beginner, it’s worth buying.
The quality is okay, but it can’t compare to the top-of-the-line versions of the “King Kong” products. I saw the prices in the mall before, and they were at least several hundred dollars lower. This is my first time playing the “King Kong” game, so it’s still quite decent.
Where is the product anti-counterfeiting mark?
The corner at the bottom of the bottle is severely dented; this must have been caused by a powerful impact. Judging from the outer packaging bag, the extent of damage is truly concerning. After all, the packaging for food products is usually very simple, especially when the products are imported from abroad. There don’t seem to be any obvious anti-counterfeiting features either. The bottle was extremely easy to open, and the layer of honey inside the cap actually came out directly. With such an opening, there’s almost no residue or sticking at all. Is this the case with all foreign products?
As someone born in China, I kind of don’t understand this.
It should be the genuine product, right?
It also mentioned in the notification that the item would arrive around the 20th of some month; however, I received it on the 14th. This is my first time making a purchase overseas from this source. I’m just writing down my doubts – perhaps all of this is normal?
I read this book when I was in high school. I read it with a bored mindset, thinking that I would not be addicted to this kind of book, because the web novels of Xinxiang before were not very attractive to me.
And then... I fell into the pit.
According to this plot, the whole book is quite light and enjoyable. In the earlier parts, when Ye Xiu is still playing in the online game, even the scheming and power struggles between guilds don’t become too serious because Ye Xiu’s antics always make things seem comical or absurd.
As for the part about the actual competition, I expressed a slight sense of sadness regarding the changes of the times and the transition from the old to the new (maybe I’m just being too sentimental).
It took me a lot of study time during my second year of high school to read the entire book, but I just couldn’t stop.
The in-depth portrayal of characters and their emotional relationships in “Full-time” is truly remarkable. Even characters that are only briefly mentioned have a profound impact on the story. Key characters like Ye Xiu, Huang Shaotian, and Su Mucheng have a huge number of fans, while those who are not at the center of the narrative, such as Jiang Botao and Chu Yunxiu, are also very likable. It’s astonishing that, within a story set in the world of esports, there isn’t a single character who is truly detestable. This is a testament to the success of “Full-time” in terms of character development.
On the emotional front, being a full-time player doesn’t incur the problems associated with the typical online novels, such as having an enormous harem of thirty million women—either all beautiful or of a perverted nature. The emotions of all the characters in the story share one common aspect: they all love the game “Glory,” and most of them desire to achieve a legitimate, honorable victory.
There aren’t any big conspiracies involved in the small measurements; everyone just looks so innocent and adorable.
This might also be why there aren’t any particularly annoying characters in the full-time roles.
The full-time position related to IP (Intellectual Property) is quite significant, and this is also thanks to that.
Putting aside the cultural phenomenon of online novels, “Full-Time” is indeed a decent book.
How much of him really gives some blood and a little bit of emotion to people?
I will be full-time in animation this April.
